# Plugin runtime state (never committed; lives in ~/.claude/ai-mentor/)
ai-mentor/state.json

# Superpowers brainstorming visual companion mockups
.superpowers/

# AI-process scaffolding: session handoffs, build plans, design specs, reviews,
# and per-issue program records. This repo is PUBLIC and ships plugins; the
# process exhaust that produced them stays local from here on.
#
# NOTE this does NOT unpublish anything — every one of these files remains in
# git history and is still readable there. Per the boundary doctrine this repo
# authored, public -> private is impossible; this governs what ships forward.
#
# docs/conventions/ is deliberately EXCLUDED below: it is shipped convention, not
# process exhaust. Each file's header states how that file is delivered.
docs/agent-driven-program/
docs/superpowers/
docs/archive/
docs/HANDOFF-*.md
docs/PLAN-*.md
docs/SPEC-*.md
docs/MIGRATION-*.md
docs/REPORT-*.md
docs/discussion.md
docs/wabash-reference-working-project.md

# OpenCode project runtime metadata and dependencies
.opencode/.gitignore
.opencode/node_modules/
.opencode/package.json
.opencode/package-lock.json
.opencode/bun.lock

# scaffold-onboard test fixtures (generated at test-run time)
scaffold-onboard/tests/fixtures/

# Local Claude Code session config and notes. Untracked AND ignored: this repo is
# PUBLIC, and .claude/ has held third-party transcripts that a stray `git add .`
# would have published. Process notes belong in the private AI workspace.
#
# ANCHORED to the repo root. An unanchored `.claude/` matches at ANY depth, which would
# shadow the 19 tracked fixture files under paths like
# claude-security-audit/fixtures/*/.claude/ and scaffold-onboard/examples/sample-project/.claude/.
# Already-tracked files are exempt from ignore rules, so nothing would have disappeared —
# but every NEW file added beside them would have been silently skipped by `git add .`,
# which is the same untracked-and-invisible failure this rule exists to prevent.
/.claude/

# ossify's own worktree directory. `oss_worktree_add` creates git worktrees under
# `<repo>/.worktrees/<work-item-id>`, and this repo — which SHIPS ossify — was not
# ignoring them. A `git add -A` here stages the worktree as a GITLINK (mode 160000):
# a submodule pointer to a branch that exists only on one machine, which clones as
# an empty directory and which nothing can resolve. Caught by doing exactly that on
# PR #179 and having to unpick it.
#
# Anchored, for the same reason `/.claude/` is: an unanchored pattern would also
# hide any `.worktrees` fixture a test creates under a plugin subtree.
/.worktrees/

# Types the Claude Code engine writes beside a loaded mod (rewritten on every load).
*/.claude-plugin/types/

# autonomic decision ledger: AI trace, which lives in the AI workspace (spec D6); never commit it here
.autonomic/
